                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Disclaimer: The Steam version of this game is severely bugged, at least (apparently?) when playing above 1080p. I was forced to refund the game after around 30 minutes because I literally couldn't progress past the second level, where a necessary platform constantly popped in and out of existence. So while I will tag this game as recommended, I refer to the PS Vita version of the game, where I ended up playing it.\n\nThis is a beautifully melancholic little game about a girl waking up in unfamiliar ruins and being guided out by two fireflies. And this is also what makes it so unique: you don't control the girl, you control the firefly, and the girl follows wherever you go - and you need to be extremely careful, because she will literally follow you to her death. And death is around every corner. The second firefly is activated on button press, and its unique ability is moving in the shadows, and being used to activate mechanisms such as levers. This results in a delightfully creative, if a bit clunky puzzle platformer that gets surprisingly challenging at times - especially if you tackle the achievements, which task you with clearing levels under special conditions. You can expect around 4 hours of gameplay for a normal playthrough, and around double that (depending on skill level) for completion.\n\nWith a depressing, but very heartfelt show-don't-tell story and an ambient soundtrack that, while not winning any awards, fits the game's scenes like a glove, htoL#NiQ is a somewhat flawed but incredibly memorable game that is well worth experiencing.",
